site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:7597:            "The official page says fiscal 2026 release schedule was published on 2026-01-30; the survey-results page lists April 2026 monthly Basic Tabulation released on 2026-05-29; e-Stat table 1-a-1 covers major items including Unemployment rate, survey date 2026Apr, update date 2026-05-29, since Jan. 1953.",
site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8131:        "80 percent interval is judgmental but tied to recent absolute monthly changes and JOLTS survey volatility",
site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8547:          text: "Model prior: I use a simple persistence plus mean-reversion prior, not a fitted time-series model, because this fast run has few fetched points and JOLTS first prints have substantial survey noise that would make a lightly specified fitted model overconfident.",
site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8555:          text: "Counter-consideration: JOLTS openings can swing sharply on survey noise and industry composition. If April's spike was genuine vacancy rebuilding, May could print above 8.0 million; if it was sampling noise or employers pulled postings after tariff and demand uncertainty, a print near 6.6 million is plausible.",
site/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:18266:          call: "Checked the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the survey month.",
