records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t23-47-40z-nbb-business-barometer-overall-2026-07/codex_stdout.jsonl:54:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n \\\"Belgium.*confidence|confidence.*Belgium|barometer|survey\\\" records site/src/data -S -g '*.json' -g '*.ts'\"","aggregated_output":"site/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:751:            \"https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/cps.html\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:954:            \"https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/cps.html\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:1163:        text: \"The FOMC SEP median, CBO projection, and the brier structural-VAR all cluster between 4.27 and 4.40 for full-year 2026. December prints tend to run very slightly below the annual mean in expansions due to seasonal adjustment behavior in the household survey.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5749:        \"Generic survey volatility\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5774:        \"The pack run leans slightly positive because employment trend and hours do not confirm a sharp break, but keeps a large lower tail for survey noise.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5779:        \"survey_release_noise\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5798:        \"Generic household-survey volatility\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:5801:        \"The control run persists April's rounded unemployment rate and gives one to two ticks of household-survey noise.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:6969:        \"Household-survey noise\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:6979:          \"No-pack unemployment-rate forecast using persistence and generic survey noise.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:6993:          text: \"The rate has clustered at 4.3 percent, so the control repeats the modal value but keeps a wider interval for household-survey noise.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-cells.ts:7465:      // Survey balances (NBB barometer, consumer confidence) print signed.\nsite/src/data/ledger-targets.ts:489:      `household-survey Table A-19 count of employed people, not seasonally ` +\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/canada-australia.ts:394:              \"{ point: 4.5, ci80: [4.2, 4.8], context: ['April unemployment 4.5%; trend 4.3%', 'April employment -18.6k', 'May release delayed to June 25 for quality assurance during survey modernisation'] }\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/canada-australia.ts:398:          \"April's seasonally adjusted rate rose to 4.5% while the trend rate remained 4.3%. The agent centers on 4.5% because a reversal is possible, but survey transition risk and the April rise argue against aggressively fading it.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/canada-australia.ts:453:            call: 'canadaAustraliaIndicatorAgent.predict({ slugs: [\"australia-employment-change-may-2026\"], sources: [\"ABS Labour Force April 2026\", \"ABS survey modernisation note\"], runAt: \"2026-06-04T11:36:25+01:00\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/occupation-employment.ts:435:      \"The June forecast stays near the May 2026 CPS table value because this monthly household-survey row is noisy and not seasonally adjusted. The point allows a small recovery from May but keeps the interval wide enough for sampling movement and month-specific occupation coding shifts.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/health.ts:95:        text: \"Direct-purchase coverage is the Census-side counterpart to marketplace enrollment. It tests whether agents can reconcile administrative sign-ups with survey-reported insurance categories.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-defense.ts:90:          \"The latest public CES path is rising steadily, up 7.8k from June 2025 to April 2026 and 5.8k since January. The forecast extends that trend but shrinks toward persistence because aerospace hiring is lumpy and the CES first print can revise around survey timing.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/agent-runs.ts:18:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/agent-runs.ts:72:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/agent-runs.ts:151:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:17:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:77:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:85:      \"Payrolls are crowded, but that is useful: Thesis can score itself against survey medians and show whether agents add value beyond consensus.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:100:      call: 'bls.lookup({ survey: \"CES\", series: \"total_nonfarm_change\", months: [\"2026-01\", \"2026-04\"], vintage: \"first_print\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:143:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:151:      \"The unemployment rate pairs with payrolls but has different noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:153:      \"Household survey employment\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:166:      call: 'bls.lookup({ survey: \"CPS\", series: \"unemployment_rate_sa\", months: [\"2026-01\", \"2026-04\"], vintage: \"first_print\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:207:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:225:      call: 'bls.lookup({ survey: \"CPI\", series: \"CPI-U all items SA MoM\", months: [\"2026-01\", \"2026-04\"], vintage: \"first_print\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:293:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:310:      call: 'census.lookup({ survey: \"MARTS\", series: \"adv44X72\", months: [\"2026-03\", \"2026-04\"], vintage: \"advance\" })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:333:            call: 'census.lookup({ survey: \"MARTS advance\", series: \"adv44X72\", fields: [\"mar2026_level\", \"apr2026_level\", \"apr_mom\", \"may_release\"] })',\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/launch-cadence.ts:354:    benchmark: \"Published consensus survey medians\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:170:      \"LFS survey uncertainty\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/uk.ts:320:      \"PAYE payrolls are a timely administrative labour-market signal, often cleaner for near-term movement than the survey unemployment rate.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/euro-japan.ts:442:      \"Monthly survey volatility\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/benefits-delivery.ts:3993:        \"text\": \"Reference class — Arkansas 2018 and the unwinding, quantified: Arkansas was the only prior real-world Medicaid work requirement at scale. Sommers et al. (NEJM 2019, telephone survey n=5,955) found 33% of the target population 'had not heard anything' about the requirement, worse among the less educated; ~18,000 lost coverage in months before a court halted it — the confusion-driven call channel. The 2023–24 unwinding is the volume analogue: a system-wide eligibility-rule shock added ~3.9M calls/month at peak and pushed 14 states above 30-minute waits. The work requirement layers a new verification touchpoint on top of the renewal baseline, with Arkansas-style confusion amplifying calls per touchpoint.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/global-near-term.ts:80:          \"The advance indicator points to a 0.6% April increase after March's 0.9% current-dollar gain. The interval allows the early estimate to revise because the advance response rate was well below the final survey response rate and March volume sales were weaker than nominal sales.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:629:        \"call\": \"ABS Labour Force survey-modernisation quality-assurance article, using inspected public Thesis record derived from ABS official sources\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:646:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: the June survey period, 31 May to 13 June, could still catch slower hiring or higher participation, and the ABS modernisation transition runs through April-August 2026. Those factors keep a 4.7 or 4.8 print in the 80 percent upper tail even though the modal rounded print remains 4.4.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:654:        \"text\": \"Upside risk: a renewed participation rise or weaker June employment gain pushes the rounded rate to 4.6-4.8. Downside risk: May's employment strength persists and unemployment falls back toward 4.2-4.3. Outside the interval scenarios are a survey-noise or labour-demand shock producing 4.0 or lower, or a sharp rise in unemployed people producing 4.9 or higher.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1961:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior uses latest official May 2026 openings of 7.594 million; adjustment components are -0.14 million for weak June payrolls/revisions, -0.01 million for lower May hires, and +0.01 million for still-low unemployment, giving 7.45 million after rounding. Interval method uses realized JOLTS first-print monthly dispersion around 0.35 to 0.40 million, widened to about +/-0.60 million for survey noise and the elevated May level, implying 6.85 to 8.05 million.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:1965:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration and falsification: upside risk is that employers keep vacancies posted even while slowing fills, which could keep openings above 7.9 million. Downside risk is that the weak June payroll print reflects a broad pullback in postings, pulling openings toward 6.9 million. A shock or survey-noise print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million would land outside the interval.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2151:        \"call\": \"Opened BLS Table A-1 for seasonally adjusted unemployment-rate history and household-survey levels.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2160:        \"text\": \"Level, momentum, one-off, and policy mechanisms: the level is low by historical standards but not accelerating lower; payroll momentum weakened sharply in June with only +57,000 jobs; the one-off risk is household-survey and participation noise after a 0.3 percentage point participation-rate drop; there is no direct policy rule that mechanically fixes July U-3 before the CPS survey.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2168:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: an upside scenario above the interval would require a July household-survey unemployment jump, such as layoffs or a labor-force reentry surge pushing the rounded rate to 4.6 percent or higher. A downside scenario below the interval would require another large participation drop or unusually strong household employment pushing the rounded rate to 4.0 percent or lower.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2362:        \"call\": \"Read BLS Table B-1 for the latest establishment-survey levels and current revised changes.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2374:        \"call\": \"Read the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the July survey month.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/july-2026-wave.ts:2391:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: I use a persistence plus short-moving-average model prior built from the February-June first-print sample, then adjust for latest revised momentum, low claims, the June one-off leisure weakness, and the softer 12-month BLS average; the 80% interval is sized from the BLS stated establishment-survey monthly-change sampling uncertainty of roughly +/-122 thousand at 90 percent, widened to +/-150 thousand for first-print and turning-point risk.\"\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/june-2026-wave.ts:1560:      \"Labor force participation and household-survey noise can move the rounded rate by 0.1pp month to month.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:658:        \"ABS flagged two incoming rotation groups for May, increasing survey-noise risk\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:801:          text: \"Counter-consideration: April's unemployment-rate rise to 4.5% and the ABS note that May has two incoming rotation groups raise downside and survey-volatility risk, so I keep a wide interval that includes another negative employment change.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:959:          call: \"ABS trend and survey-timing context from April 2026 Labour Force release\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:961:            \"Fetched April 2026 trend unemployment rate 4.3%, trend unemployed people 669,500, seasonally adjusted unemployed people 692,500, and May 2026 survey reference weeks 3 May 2026 to 16 May 2026.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:3714:          \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/health-insurance/time-series/hic/hhi01.xlsx\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:4016:                \"The 80% interval is justified mainly from two recent annual changes plus qualitative survey/category noise, which is a weak realized-volatility basis.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:4138:          text: \"Base 2024 rate 10.8 + expected 2025 net increase 0.5 = 11.3. The 80 percent interval is judgmental rather than a clean long-history volatility estimate: recent annual changes of +0.3 and +0.6, survey/category noise, and policy churn support a +/-0.6 point band, giving 10.7 to 11.9.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:6762:            \"The official schedule lists Japan survey month May with date of release June 19, 2026; the page last update is 23 January 2026.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:6822:        \"One-off calendar and bonus-timing effects can move the monthly household survey materially\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:7432:            \"Schedule shows Ku-area of Tokyo preliminary: survey month May 2026 release May 29 and survey month June 2026 release June 26; same schedule line shows Japan May release June 19.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:7595:          call: \"Checked the Statistics Bureau Labour Force Survey landing and survey-results pages for the official series surface and latest monthly release status.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:7597:            \"The official page says fiscal 2026 release schedule was published on 2026-01-30; the survey-results page lists April 2026 monthly Basic Tabulation released on 2026-05-29; e-Stat table 1-a-1 covers major items including Unemployment rate, survey date 2026Apr, update date 2026-05-29, since Jan. 1953.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8131:        \"80 percent interval is judgmental but tied to recent absolute monthly changes and JOLTS survey volatility\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8547:          text: \"Model prior: I use a simple persistence plus mean-reversion prior, not a fitted time-series model, because this fast run has few fetched points and JOLTS first prints have substantial survey noise that would make a lightly specified fitted model overconfident.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:8555:          text: \"Counter-consideration: JOLTS openings can swing sharply on survey noise and industry composition. If April's spike was genuine vacancy rebuilding, May could print above 8.0 million; if it was sampling noise or employers pulled postings after tariff and demand uncertainty, a print near 6.6 million is plausible.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:18266:          call: \"Checked the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the survey month.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:20770:          \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:20772:          \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:21225:          \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:21526:                \"The 80% interval is asserted as roughly -1.0/+1.2 points but is not tied to realized volatility, backtest error, survey error, or a stated subjective uncertainty calculation.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:21649:          text: \"Counter-consideration: if 2025 shelter and medical out-of-pocket adjustments run hotter than cash-income gains, or if survey income measurement weakens, the rate could stay near 13.5 or above despite continued employment growth.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:21653:          text: \"Point estimate: start from the 2022-2024 average (12.4+12.9+12.9)/3=12.73, apply only a small -0.1 persistence-plus-mild-income adjustment, and round to 12.6. For the 80% interval, recent non-pandemic-transfer year-to-year moves include 2022 to 2023 of +0.5 and 2023 to 2024 of 0.0, but SPM is sensitive to thresholds, expenses, survey measurement, and policy mechanics, so I use a subjective central interval of about -1.0/+1.2 percentage points around the point: 11.6 to 13.8.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22101:          text: \"Counter-consideration: LFS estimates remain volatile and ONS warns against over-reading short-term movements; May payroll was nearly flat after the April drop, so a continuation at 4.9 or a dip to 4.8 remains plausible if survey composition offsets payroll weakness.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22109:          text: \"Upside scenario: April-June layoffs and hiring freezes show through clearly in the LFS and unemployment prints 5.4 or 5.5. Downside scenario: April payroll weakness is revised away and survey volatility holds unemployment near 4.8 or 4.9. Outside-the-interval scenarios require either a sharp labour-market break above 5.5 or a strong participation/employment surprise below 4.7.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22542:          text: \"Policy and measurement mechanism: the target is a household-survey unemployment rate, not a claimant-count or payroll series. Soft PAYE and vacancies tend to lift unemployment with a lag, but seasonal-adjustment review and LFS sampling variability can move the first print by several tenths without a true labour-market break.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22721:          text: \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22725:          text: \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22750:        \"Household-survey sampling noise can move the rounded rate by a few tenths\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22873:          call: \"Read BLS archived May 2026 Employment Situation table A-1 for recent household-survey values.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22890:          text: \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\",\nsite/src/data/thesis-analyst-live-comparisons.ts:22898:          text: \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:297:      \"Manufacturing survey momentum\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:337:          \"April's 0.7% gain was helped by manufacturing, motor vehicles, and utilities. The May forecast fades the utilities impulse and keeps the center modestly positive because manufacturing excluding vehicles still grew in April, while mining and survey data keep a meaningful downside tail.\",\nsite/src/data/forecast-examples/us-near-term.ts:537:          \"Starts fell modestly in April while permits rose, giving mixed short-run signals. The forecast centers slightly below April because mortgage-rate pressure and single-family softness outweigh the permit rebound, but survey noise keeps the band wide.\",\nrecords/brier-judges/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-38-10z-reasoning-judge/judge.json:158:      \"proposedChange\": \"Add target metadata such as release_series, statutory_formula, vintage_revision, first_print_page, and survey_sampling. Use it to select public_release_fast, formula_nowcast, vintage_series, or survey_nowcast prompt modes.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/normalized_cells.json:77:    \"call\": \"Opened BLS Table A-1 for seasonally adjusted unemployment-rate history and household-survey levels.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/normalized_cells.json:86:    \"text\": \"Level, momentum, one-off, and policy mechanisms: the level is low by historical standards but not accelerating lower; payroll momentum weakened sharply in June with only +57,000 jobs; the one-off risk is household-survey and participation noise after a 0.3 percentage point participation-rate drop; there is no direct policy rule that mechanically fixes July U-3 before the CPS survey.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/normalized_cells.json:94:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: an upside scenario above the interval would require a July household-survey unemployment jump, such as layoffs or a labor-force reentry surge pushing the rounded rate to 4.6 percent or higher. A downside scenario below the interval would require another large participation drop or unusually strong household employment pushing the rounded rate to 4.0 percent or lower.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-44-24z-statjp-cpi-tokyo-all-items-yoy-2026-07/normalized_cells.json:85:    \"result\": \"Fetched e-Stat file-list metadata: Table 1-2 Subgroup Index for Ku-area of Tokyo, Monthly, survey date 2026 May, update date 2026-06-26; Table 4-2 Indices of Items for Ku-area of Tokyo, Monthly, survey date 2026 May, update date 2026-06-26.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/parsed_cells.json:77:        \"call\": \"Opened BLS Table A-1 for seasonally adjusted unemployment-rate history and household-survey levels.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/parsed_cells.json:86:        \"text\": \"Level, momentum, one-off, and policy mechanisms: the level is low by historical standards but not accelerating lower; payroll momentum weakened sharply in June with only +57,000 jobs; the one-off risk is household-survey and participation noise after a 0.3 percentage point participation-rate drop; there is no direct policy rule that mechanically fixes July U-3 before the CPS survey.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/parsed_cells.json:94:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: an upside scenario above the interval would require a July household-survey unemployment jump, such as layoffs or a labor-force reentry surge pushing the rounded rate to 4.6 percent or higher. A downside scenario below the interval would require another large participation drop or unusually strong household employment pushing the rounded rate to 4.0 percent or lower.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/cells.with_activity.json:77:        \"call\": \"Opened BLS Table A-1 for seasonally adjusted unemployment-rate history and household-survey levels.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/cells.with_activity.json:86:        \"text\": \"Level, momentum, one-off, and policy mechanisms: the level is low by historical standards but not accelerating lower; payroll momentum weakened sharply in June with only +57,000 jobs; the one-off risk is household-survey and participation noise after a 0.3 percentage point participation-rate drop; there is no direct policy rule that mechanically fixes July U-3 before the CPS survey.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-09-26z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-07/cells.with_activity.json:94:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: an upside scenario above the interval would require a July household-survey unemployment jump, such as layoffs or a labor-force reentry surge pushing the rounded rate to 4.6 percent or higher. A downside scenario below the interval would require another large participation drop or unusually strong household employment pushing the rounded rate to 4.0 percent or lower.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-44-24z-statjp-cpi-tokyo-all-items-yoy-2026-07/parsed_c