month.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-38-59z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-07/cells.with_activity.json:110:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: I use a persistence plus short-moving-average model prior built from the February-June first-print sample, then adjust for latest revised momentum, low claims, the June one-off leisure weakness, and the softer 12-month BLS average; the 80% interval is sized from the BLS stated establishment-survey monthly-change sampling uncertainty of roughly +/-122 thousand at 90 percent, widened to +/-150 thousand for first-print and turning-point risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:82:    \"call\": \"ABS Labour Force survey-modernisation quality-assurance article\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:95:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: the June survey period, 31 May to 13 June, could still catch slower hiring or higher participation, and the ABS modernisation transition runs through April-August 2026. Those factors keep a 4.7 or 4.8 print in the 80 percent upper tail even though the modal rounded print remains 4.4.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:103:    \"text\": \"Upside scenario: a renewed participation rise or weaker June employment gain pushes the rounded rate to 4.6-4.8. Downside scenario: May's employment strength persists and unemployment falls back toward 4.2-4.3. Outside-the-interval scenarios are a survey-noise or labour-demand shock producing 4.0 or lower, or a sharp rise in unemployed people producing 4.9 or higher.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-03-35z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:85:    \"call\": \"ABS trend and survey-timing context from April 2026 Labour Force release\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-03-35z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:86:    \"result\": \"Fetched April 2026 trend unemployment rate 4.3%, trend unemployed people 669,500, seasonally adjusted unemployed people 692,500, and May 2026 survey reference weeks 3 May 2026 to 16 May 2026.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-03-35z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-05/parsed_cells.json:85:        \"call\": \"ABS trend and survey-timing context from April 2026 Labour Force release\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-03-35z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-05/parsed_cells.json:86:        \"result\": \"Fetched April 2026 trend unemployment rate 4.3%, trend unemployed people 669,500, seasonally adjusted unemployed people 692,500, and May 2026 survey reference weeks 3 May 2026 to 16 May 2026.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:82:        \"call\": \"ABS Labour Force survey-modernisation quality-assurance article\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:95:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: the June survey period, 31 May to 13 June, could still catch slower hiring or higher participation, and the ABS modernisation transition runs through April-August 2026. Those factors keep a 4.7 or 4.8 print in the 80 percent upper tail even though the modal rounded print remains 4.4.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:103:        \"text\": \"Upside scenario: a renewed participation rise or weaker June employment gain pushes the rounded rate to 4.6-4.8. Downside scenario: May's employment strength persists and unemployment falls back toward 4.2-4.3. Outside-the-interval scenarios are a survey-noise or labour-demand shock producing 4.0 or lower, or a sharp rise in unemployed people producing 4.9 or higher.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:82:        \"call\": \"ABS Labour Force survey-modernisation quality-assurance article\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:95:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: the June survey period, 31 May to 13 June, could still catch slower hiring or higher participation, and the ABS modernisation transition runs through April-August 2026. Those factors keep a 4.7 or 4.8 print in the 80 percent upper tail even though the modal rounded print remains 4.4.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:103:        \"text\": \"Upside scenario: a renewed participation rise or weaker June employment gain pushes the rounded rate to 4.6-4.8. Downside scenario: May's employment strength persists and unemployment falls back toward 4.2-4.3. Outside-the-interval scenarios are a survey-noise or labour-demand shock producing 4.0 or lower, or a sharp rise in unemployed people producing 4.9 or higher.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-03-35z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:85:        \"call\": \"ABS trend and survey-timing context from April 2026 Labour Force release\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-03-35z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:86:        \"result\": \"Fetched April 2026 trend unemployment rate 4.3%, trend unemployed people 669,500, seasonally adjusted unemployed people 692,500, and May 2026 survey reference weeks 3 May 2026 to 16 May 2026.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-18-26z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:100:    \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior uses latest official May 2026 openings of 7.594 million; adjustment components are -0.14 million for weak June payrolls/revisions, -0.01 million for lower May hires, and +0.01 million for still-low unemployment, giving 7.45 million after rounding. Interval method uses realized JOLTS first-print monthly dispersion around 0.35 to 0.40 million, widened to about +/-0.60 million for survey noise and the elevated May level, implying 6.85 to 8.05 million.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-18-26z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:104:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration and falsification: upside risk is that employers keep vacancies posted even while slowing fills, which could keep openings above 7.9 million. Downside risk is that the weak June payroll print reflects a broad pullback in postings, pulling openings toward 6.9 million. A shock or survey-noise print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million would land outside the interval.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-06-42z-statjp-cpi-tokyo-all-items-yoy-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:68:    \"result\": \"Schedule shows Ku-area of Tokyo preliminary: survey month May 2026 release May 29 and survey month June 2026 release June 26; same schedule line shows Japan May release June 19.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-11-22z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:114:    \"text\": \"Upside scenario: openings stay near or above 7.9 million if employers keep requisitions open despite slower hiring and labor-supply constraints bind in services. Downside scenario: openings fall toward 6.8 million if June's payroll slowdown reflects a rapid pullback in vacancy posting. Outside-the-interval scenario: a survey-noise or broad-demand shock print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million is possible but less likely than normal JOLTS volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-06-42z-statjp-cpi-tokyo-all-items-yoy-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:68:        \"result\": \"Schedule shows Ku-area of Tokyo preliminary: survey month May 2026 release May 29 and survey month June 2026 release June 26; same schedule line shows Japan May release June 19.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-18-26z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:100:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior uses latest official May 2026 openings of 7.594 million; adjustment components are -0.14 million for weak June payrolls/revisions, -0.01 million for lower May hires, and +0.01 million for still-low unemployment, giving 7.45 million after rounding. Interval method uses realized JOLTS first-print monthly dispersion around 0.35 to 0.40 million, widened to about +/-0.60 million for survey noise and the elevated May level, implying 6.85 to 8.05 million.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-18-26z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:104:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration and falsification: upside risk is that employers keep vacancies posted even while slowing fills, which could keep openings above 7.9 million. Downside risk is that the weak June payroll print reflects a broad pullback in postings, pulling openings toward 6.9 million. A shock or survey-noise print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million would land outside the interval.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-11-22z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:114:        \"text\": \"Upside scenario: openings stay near or above 7.9 million if employers keep requisitions open despite slower hiring and labor-supply constraints bind in services. Downside scenario: openings fall toward 6.8 million if June's payroll slowdown reflects a rapid pullback in vacancy posting. Outside-the-interval scenario: a survey-noise or broad-demand shock print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million is possible but less likely than normal JOLTS volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-11-22z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:114:        \"text\": \"Upside scenario: openings stay near or above 7.9 million if employers keep requisitions open despite slower hiring and labor-supply constraints bind in services. Downside scenario: openings fall toward 6.8 million if June's payroll slowdown reflects a rapid pullback in vacancy posting. Outside-the-interval scenario: a survey-noise or broad-demand shock print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million is possible but less likely than normal JOLTS volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-06-42z-statjp-cpi-tokyo-all-items-yoy-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:68:        \"result\": \"Schedule shows Ku-area of Tokyo preliminary: survey month May 2026 release May 29 and survey month June 2026 release June 26; same schedule line shows Japan May release June 19.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-18-26z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:100:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior uses latest official May 2026 openings of 7.594 million; adjustment components are -0.14 million for weak June payrolls/revisions, -0.01 million for lower May hires, and +0.01 million for still-low unemployment, giving 7.45 million after rounding. Interval method uses realized JOLTS first-print monthly dispersion around 0.35 to 0.40 million, widened to about +/-0.60 million for survey noise and the elevated May level, implying 6.85 to 8.05 million.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-18-26z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:104:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration and falsification: upside risk is that employers keep vacancies posted even while slowing fills, which could keep openings above 7.9 million. Downside risk is that the weak June payroll print reflects a broad pullback in postings, pulling openings toward 6.9 million. A shock or survey-noise print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million would land outside the interval.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-21-05z-test-series-2030-01/normalized_cells.json:69:    \"text\": \"Outside the interval if hiring weakens abruptly or the survey mean-reverts faster than the recent prints imply.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-21-05z-test-series-2030-01/cells.with_activity.json:69:        \"text\": \"Outside the interval if hiring weakens abruptly or the survey mean-reverts faster than the recent prints imply.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-29-00z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:82:    \"call\": \"ABS Labour Force survey-modernisation quality-assurance article, using inspected public Thesis record derived from ABS official sources\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-29-00z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:99:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: the June survey period, 31 May to 13 June, could still catch slower hiring or higher participation, and the ABS modernisation transition runs through April-August 2026. Those factors keep a 4.7 or 4.8 print in the 80 percent upper tail even though the modal rounded print remains 4.4.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-29-00z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:107:    \"text\": \"Upside risk: a renewed participation rise or weaker June employment gain pushes the rounded rate to 4.6-4.8. Downside risk: May's employment strength persists and unemployment falls back toward 4.2-4.3. Outside the interval scenarios are a survey-noise or labour-demand shock producing 4.0 or lower, or a sharp rise in unemployed people producing 4.9 or higher.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-04-41z-abs-labour-employment-change-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:41:   \"ABS flagged two incoming rotation groups for May, increasing survey-noise risk\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-04-41z-abs-labour-employment-change-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:92:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: April's unemployment-rate rise to 4.5% and the ABS note that May has two incoming rotation groups raise downside and survey-volatility risk, so I keep a wide interval that includes another negative employment change.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t01-50-58z-estat-jp-cpi-core-exfreshfood-yoy-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:64:    \"result\": \"The official schedule lists Japan survey month May with date of release June 19, 2026; the page last update is 23 January 2026.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-21-05z-test-series-2030-01/parsed_cells.json:69:        \"text\": \"Outside the interval if hiring weakens abruptly or the survey mean-reverts faster than the recent prints imply.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-29-00z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:82:        \"call\": \"ABS Labour Force survey-modernisation quality-assurance article, using inspected public Thesis record derived from ABS official sources\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-29-00z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:99:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: the June survey period, 31 May to 13 June, could still catch slower hiring or higher participation, and the ABS modernisation transition runs through April-August 2026. Those factors keep a 4.7 or 4.8 print in the 80 percent upper tail even though the modal rounded print remains 4.4.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-29-00z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:107:        \"text\": \"Upside risk: a renewed participation rise or weaker June employment gain pushes the rounded rate to 4.6-4.8. Downside risk: May's employment strength persists and unemployment falls back toward 4.2-4.3. Outside the interval scenarios are a survey-noise or labour-demand shock producing 4.0 or lower, or a sharp rise in unemployed people producing 4.9 or higher.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t01-50-58z-estat-jp-cpi-core-exf