"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-job-openings-and-labor-turnover-survey-u-s-bureau.11bcf560\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:30069:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-job-openings-and-labor-turnover-survey-u-s-bureau.11bcf560\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:30300:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.census-asec-current-population-survey-census-asec-current-population-survey.d22ad152\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:30311:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.census-asec-current-population-survey-census-asec-current-population-survey.d22ad152\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:30322:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.census-asec-current-population-survey-census-asec-current-population-survey.d22ad152\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:30333:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.census-asec-current-population-survey-census-asec-current-population-survey.d22ad152\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:42917:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:42928:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:42939:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:42950:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:42961:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:42972:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:58822:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.census-asec-current-population-survey-census-asec-current-population-survey.d22ad152\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:58830:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.census-asec-current-population-survey-census-asec-current-population-survey.d22ad152\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:68118:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-job-openings-and-labor-turnover-survey-u-s-bureau.11bcf560\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:68126:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-job-openings-and-labor-turnover-survey-u-s-bureau.11bcf560\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:68166:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:68174:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:76282:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-job-openings-and-labor-turnover-survey-u-s-bureau.11bcf560\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:76348:      \"sourceSeriesId\": \"source_series.u-s-bureau-of-labor-statistics-employment-situation-current-population-survey-u-.db09efde\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2147992:      \"publicText\": \"The FOMC SEP median, CBO projection, and the brier structural-VAR all cluster between 4.27 and 4.40 for full-year 2026. December prints tend to run very slightly below the annual mean in expansions due to seasonal adjustment behavior in the household survey.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2149328:      \"publicText\": \"Counter-consideration: if 2025 shelter and medical out-of-pocket adjustments run hotter than cash-income gains, or if survey income measurement weakens, the rate could stay near 13.5 or above despite continued employment growth.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150944:      \"publicText\": \"Payrolls are crowded, but that is useful: Thesis can score itself against survey medians and show whether agents add value beyond consensus. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2150976:      \"publicText\": \"The unemployment rate pairs with payrolls but has different noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2151584:      \"publicText\": \"Model prior: I use a simple persistence plus mean-reversion prior, not a fitted time-series model, because this fast run has few fetched points and JOLTS first prints have substantial survey noise that would make a lightly specified fitted model overconfident.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152504:      \"publicText\": \"Counter-consideration: LFS estimates remain volatile and ONS warns against over-reading short-term movements; May payroll was nearly flat after the April drop, so a continuation at 4.9 or a dip to 4.8 remains plausible if survey composition offsets payroll weakness.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152616:      \"publicText\": \"Policy and measurement mechanism: the target is a household-survey unemployment rate, not a claimant-count or payroll series. Soft PAYE and vacancies tend to lift unemployment with a lag, but seasonal-adjustment review and LFS sampling variability can move the first print by several tenths without a true labour-market break.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2152712:      \"publicText\": \"PAYE payrolls are a timely administrative labour-market signal, often cleaner for near-term movement than the survey unemployment rate. This target resolves on 2026-06-18 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~2 weeks lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, labour-market threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2153184:      \"publicText\": \"April's seasonally adjusted rate rose to 4.5% while the trend rate remained 4.3%. The agent centers on 4.5% because a reversal is possible, but survey transition risk and the April rise argue against aggressively fading it.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2153208:      \"publicText\": \"The control run persists April's rounded unemployment rate and gives one to two ticks of household-survey noise.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2153360:      \"publicText\": \"The pack run leans slightly positive because employment trend and hours do not confirm a sharp break, but keeps a large lower tail for survey noise.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2153408:      \"publicText\": \"Counter-consideration: April's unemployment-rate rise to 4.5% and the ABS note that May has two incoming rotation groups raise downside and survey-volatility risk, so I keep a wide interval that includes another negative employment change.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2154200:      \"publicText\": \"April's 0.7% gain was helped by manufacturing, motor vehicles, and utilities. The May forecast fades the utilities impulse and keeps the center modestly positive because manufacturing excluding vehicles still grew in April, while mining and survey data keep a meaningful downside tail.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2154296:      \"publicText\": \"Starts fell modestly in April while permits rose, giving mixed short-run signals. The forecast centers slightly below April because mortgage-rate pressure and single-family softness outweigh the permit rebound, but survey noise keeps the band wide.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2155256:      \"publicText\": \"The latest public CES path is rising steadily, up 7.8k from June 2025 to April 2026 and 5.8k since January. The forecast extends that trend but shrinks toward persistence because aerospace hiring is lumpy and the CES first print can revise around survey timing.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2156200:      \"publicText\": \"The June forecast stays near the May 2026 CPS table value because this monthly household-survey row is noisy and not seasonally adjusted. The point allows a small recovery from May but keeps the interval wide enough for sampling movement and month-specific occupation coding shifts.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2170072:      \"publicText\": \"The advance indicator points to a 0.6% April increase after March's 0.9% current-dollar gain. The interval allows the early estimate to revise because the advance response rate was well below the final survey response rate and March volume sales were weaker than nominal sales.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2170784:      \"publicText\": \"Direct-purchase coverage is the Census-side counterpart to marketplace enrollment. It tests whether agents can reconcile administrative sign-ups with survey-reported insurance categories.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2182824:      \"publicText\": \"Reference class — Arkansas 2018 and the unwinding, quantified: Arkansas was the only prior real-world Medicaid work requirement at scale. Sommers et al. (NEJM 2019, telephone survey n=5,955) found 33% of the target population 'had not heard anything' about the requirement, worse among the less educated; ~18,000 lost coverage in months before a court halted it — the confusion-driven call channel. The 2023–24 unwinding is the volume analogue: a system-wide eligibility-rule shock added ~3.9M calls/month at peak and pushed 14 states above 30-minute waits. The work requirement layers a new verification touchpoint on top of the renewal baseline, with Arkansas-style confusion amplifying calls per touchpoint.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2189376:      \"publicText\": \"The rate has clustered at 4.3 percent, so the control repeats the modal value but keeps a wider interval for household-survey noise.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2189440:      \"publicText\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2189448:      \"publicText\": \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2189488:      \"publicText\": \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2189504:      \"publicText\": \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2190707:          \"summary\": \"The 80% interval is asserted as roughly -1.0/+1.2 points but is not tied to realized volatility, backtest error, survey error, or a stated subjective uncertainty calculation.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2191535:          \"summary\": \"The 80% interval is justified mainly from two recent annual changes plus qualitative survey/category noise, which is a weak realized-volatility basis.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2193094:            \"The FOMC SEP median, CBO projection, and the brier structural-VAR all cluster between 4.27 and 4.40 for full-year 2026. December prints tend to run very slightly below the annual mean in expansions due to seasonal adjustment behavior in the household survey.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2194899:            \"Point estimate: start from the 2022-2024 average (12.4+12.9+12.9)/3=12.73, apply only a small -0.1 persistence-plus-mild-income adjustment, and round to 12.6. For the 80% interval, recent non-pandemic-transfer year-to-year moves include 2022 to 2023 of +0.5 and 2023 to 2024 of 0.0, but SPM is sensitive to thresholds, expenses, survey measurement, and policy mechanics, so I use a subjective central interval of about -1.0/+1.2 percentage points around the point: 11.6 to 13.8.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2194919:            \"Point estimate: start from the 2022-2024 average (12.4+12.9+12.9)/3=12.73, apply only a small -0.1 persistence-plus-mild-income adjustment, and round to 12.6. For the 80% interval, recent non-pandemic-transfer year-to-year moves include 2022 to 2023 of +0.5 and 2023 to 2024 of 0.0, but SPM is sensitive to thresholds, expenses, survey measurement, and policy mechanics, so I use a subjective central interval of about -1.0/+1.2 percentage points around the point: 11.6 to 13.8.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2194930:            \"Point estimate: start from the 2022-2024 average (12.4+12.9+12.9)/3=12.73, apply only a small -0.1 persistence-plus-mild-income adjustment, and round to 12.6. For the 80% interval, recent non-pandemic-transfer year-to-year moves include 2022 to 2023 of +0.5 and 2023 to 2024 of 0.0, but SPM is sensitive to thresholds, expenses, survey measurement, and policy mechanics, so I use a subjective central interval of about -1.0/+1.2 percentage points around the point: 11.6 to 13.8.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198202:            \"Payrolls are crowded, but that is useful: Thesis can score itself against survey medians and show whether agents add value beyond consensus. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198212:            \"Payrolls are crowded, but that is useful: Thesis can score itself against survey medians and show whether agents add value beyond consensus. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198213:            \"Tool call: bls.lookup({ survey: \\\"CES\\\", series: \\\"total_nonfarm_change\\\", months: [\\\"2026-01\\\", \\\"2026-04\\\"], vintage: \\\"first_print\\\" })\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198223:            \"Payrolls are crowded, but that is useful: Thesis can score itself against survey medians and show whether agents add value beyond consensus. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198248:            \"Payrolls are crowded, but that is useful: Thesis can score itself against survey medians and show whether agents add value beyond consensus. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198288:            \"The unemployment rate pairs with payrolls but has different noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198289:            \"Tool call: bls.lookup({ survey: \\\"CPS\\\", series: \\\"unemployment_rate_sa\\\", months: [\\\"2026-01\\\", \\\"2026-04\\\"], vintage: \\\"first_print\\\" })\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2198299:            \"The unemployment rate pairs with payrolls but has different noise: household-survey employment, labor-force entry, and rounding to one decimal place. This target resolves on 2026-06-05 under a first-print rule, with an expected ~1 week lag. The same series can also spawn next release, +3 months, +12 months, threshold questions.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2