2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-04-41z-abs-labour-employment-change-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:92:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: April's unemployment-rate rise to 4.5% and the ABS note that May has two incoming rotation groups raise downside and survey-volatility risk, so I keep a wide interval that includes another negative employment change.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-06-11z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:79:        \"call\": \"Checked the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the survey month.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:93:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:97:    \"text\": \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-06-11z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:79:        \"call\": \"Checked the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the survey month.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:45:   \"Household-survey sampling noise can move the rounded rate by a few tenths\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:78:    \"call\": \"Read BLS archived May 2026 Employment Situation table A-1 for recent household-survey values.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:93:    \"text\": \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:101:    \"text\": \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:93:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:97:        \"text\": \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:93:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:97:        \"text\": \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:16:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolve to the first Statistics Bureau of Japan Labour Force Survey Basic Tabulation May 2026 seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for total persons aged 15 years or over, as first released on the official monthly results/survey results page on June 30, 2026. Use the agency's published percent value rounded to one decimal place; ignore later revisions or historical-data back revisions for this forecast cell.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:67:        \"call\": \"Checked the Statistics Bureau Labour Force Survey landing and survey-results pages for the official series surface and latest monthly release status.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:68:        \"result\": \"The official page says fiscal 2026 release schedule was published on 2026-01-30; the survey-results page lists April 2026 monthly Basic Tabulation released on 2026-05-29; e-Stat table 1-a-1 covers major items including Unemployment rate, survey date 2026Apr, update date 2026-05-29, since Jan. 1953.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-33-47z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-july-to-september-2026-first-print-jul-sep-2026/normalized_cells.json:107:    \"text\": \"Policy and measurement mechanism: the target is a household-survey unemployment rate, not a claimant-count or payroll series. Soft PAYE and vacancies tend to lift unemployment with a lag, but seasonal-adjustment review and LFS sampling variability can move the first print by several tenths without a true labour-market break.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:45:      \"Household-survey sampling noise can move the rounded rate by a few tenths\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:78:        \"call\": \"Read BLS archived May 2026 Employment Situation table A-1 for recent household-survey values.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:93:        \"text\": \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:101:        \"text\": \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:16:  \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolve to the first Statistics Bureau of Japan Labour Force Survey Basic Tabulation May 2026 seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for total persons aged 15 years or over, as first released on the official monthly results/survey results page on June 30, 2026. Use the agency's published percent value rounded to one decimal place; ignore later revisions or historical-data back revisions for this forecast cell.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:67:    \"call\": \"Checked the Statistics Bureau Labour Force Survey landing and survey-results pages for the official series surface and latest monthly release status.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:68:    \"result\": \"The official page says fiscal 2026 release schedule was published on 2026-01-30; the survey-results page lists April 2026 monthly Basic Tabulation released on 2026-05-29; e-Stat table 1-a-1 covers major items including Unemployment rate, survey date 2026Apr, update date 2026-05-29, since Jan. 1953.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:48:   \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:50:   \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:45:      \"Household-survey sampling noise can move the rounded rate by a few tenths\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:78:        \"call\": \"Read BLS archived May 2026 Employment Situation table A-1 for recent household-survey values.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:93:        \"text\": \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:101:        \"text\": \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:48:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:50:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:48:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:50:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-33-47z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-july-to-september-2026-first-print-jul-sep-2026/parsed_cells.json:107:        \"text\": \"Policy and measurement mechanism: the target is a household-survey unemployment rate, not a claimant-count or payroll series. Soft PAYE and vacancies tend to lift unemployment with a lag, but seasonal-adjustment review and LFS sampling variability can move the first print by several tenths without a true labour-market break.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-33-47z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-july-to-september-2026-first-print-jul-sep-2026/cells.with_activity.json:107:        \"text\": \"Policy and measurement mechanism: the target is a household-survey unemployment rate, not a claimant-count or payroll series. Soft PAYE and vacancies tend to lift unemployment with a lag, but seasonal-adjustment review and LFS sampling variability can move the first print by several tenths without a true labour-market break.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/normalized_cells.json:45:   \"80 percent interval is judgmental but tied to recent absolute monthly changes and JOLTS survey volatility\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/normalized_cells.json:86:    \"text\": \"Model prior: I use a simple persistence plus mean-reversion prior, not a fitted time-series model, because this fast run has few fetched points and JOLTS first prints have substantial survey noise that would make a lightly specified fitted model overconfident.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/normalized_cells.json:94:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: JOLTS openings can swing sharply on survey noise and industry composition. If April's spike was genuine vacancy rebuilding, May could print above 8.0 million; if it was sampling noise or employers pulled postings after tariff and demand uncertainty, a print near 6.6 million is plausible.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/parsed_cells.json:45:      \"80 percent interval is judgmental but tied to recent absolute monthly changes and JOLTS survey volatility\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/parsed_cells.json:86:        \"text\": \"Model prior: I use a simple persistence plus mean-reversion prior, not a fitted time-series model, because this fast run has few fetched points and JOLTS first prints have substantial survey noise that would make a lightly specified fitted model overconfident.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/parsed_cells.json:94:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: JOLTS openings can swing sharply on survey noise and industry composition. If April's spike was genuine vacancy rebuilding, May could print above 8.0 million; if it was sampling noise or employers pulled postings after tariff and demand uncertainty, a print near 6.6 million is plausible.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-34-28z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-april-to-june-2026-first-print-apr-jun-2026/normalized_cells.json:102:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: LFS estimates remain volatile and ONS warns against over-reading short-term movements; May payroll was nearly flat after the April drop, so a continuation at 4.9 or a dip to 4.8 remains plausible if survey composition offsets payroll weakness.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-34-28z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-april-to-june-2026-first-print-apr-jun-2026/normalized_cells.json:110:    \"text\": \"Upside scenario: April-June layoffs and hiring freezes show through clearly in the LFS and unemployment prints 5.4 or 5.5. Downside scenario: April payroll weakness is revised away and survey volatility holds unemployment near 4.8 or 4.9. Outside-the-interval scenarios require either a sharp labour-market break above 5.5 or a strong participation/employment surprise below 4.7.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/cells.with_activity.json:45:      \"80 percent interval is judgmental but tied to recent absolute monthly changes and JOLTS survey volatility\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/cells.with_activity.json:86:        \"text\": \"Model prior: I use a simple persistence plus mean-reversion prior, not a fitted time-series model, because this fast run has few fetched points and JOLTS first prints have substantial survey noise that would make a lightly specified fitted model overconfident.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-07-53z-bls-jolts-job-openings-total-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/cells.with_activity.json:94:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: JOLTS openings can swing sharply on survey noise and industry composition. If April's spike was genuine vacancy rebuilding, May could print above 8.0 million; if it was sampling noise or employers pulled postings after tariff and demand uncertainty, a print near 6.6 million is plausible.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/parsed_cells.json:16:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolve to the first Statistics Bureau of Japan Labour Force Survey Basic Tabulation May 2026 seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for total persons aged 15 years or over, as first released on the official monthly results/survey results page on June 30, 2026. Use the agency's published percent value rounded to one decimal place; ignore later revisions or historical-data back revisions for this forecast cell.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/parsed_cells.json:67:        \"call\": \"Checked the Statistics Bureau Labour Force Survey landing and survey-results pages for the official series surface and latest monthly release status.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/parsed_cells.json:68:        \"result\": \"The official page says fiscal 2026 release schedule was published on 2026-01-30; the survey-results page lists April 2026 monthly Basic Tabulation released on 2026-05-29; e-Stat table 1-a-1 covers major items including Unemployment rate, survey date 2026Apr, update date 2026-05-29, since Jan. 1953.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-34-28z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-april-to-june-2026-first-print-apr-jun-2026/parsed_cells.json:102:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: LFS estimates remain volatile and ONS warns against over-reading short-term movements; May payroll was nearly flat after the April drop, so a continuation at 4.9 or a dip to 4.8 remains plausible if survey composition offsets payroll weakness.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-34-28z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-april-to-june-2026-first-print-apr-jun-2026/parsed_cells.json:110:        \"text\": \"Upside scenario: April-June layoffs and hiring freezes show through clearly in the LFS and unemployment prints 5.4 or 5.5. Downside scenario: April payroll weakness is revised away and survey volatility holds unemployment near 4.8 or 4.9. Outside-the-interval scenarios require either a sharp labour-market break above 5.5 or a strong participation/employment surprise below 4.7.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-34-28z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-april-to-june-2026-first-print-apr-jun-2026/cells.with_activity.json:102:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: LFS estimates remain volatile and ONS warns against over-reading short-term movements; May payroll was nearly flat after the April drop, so a continuation at 4.9 or a dip to 4.8 remains plausible if survey composition offsets payroll weakness.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-34-28z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-april-to-june-2026-first-print-apr-jun-2026/cells.with_activity.json:110:        \"text\": \"Upside scenario: April-June layoffs and hiring freezes show through clearly in the LFS and unemployment prints 5.4 or 5.5. Downside scenario: April payroll weakness is revised away and survey volatility holds unemployment near 4.8 or 4.9. Outside-the-interval scenarios require either a sharp labour-market break above 5.5 or a strong participation/employment surprise below 4.7.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/normalized_cells.json:48:   \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/normalized_cells.json:94:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: if 2025 shelter and medical out-of-pocket adjustments run hotter than cash-income gains, or if survey income measurement weakens, the rate could stay near 13.5 or above despite continued employment growth.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/normalized_cells.json:98:    \"text\": \"Point estimate: start from the 2022-2024 average (12.4+12.9+12.9)/3=12.73, apply only a small -0.1 persistence-plus-mild-income adjustment, and round to 12.6. For the 80% interval, recent non-pandemic-transfer year-to-year moves include 2022 to 2023 of +0.5 and 2023 to 2024 of 0.0, but SPM is sensitive to thresholds, expenses, survey measurement, and policy mechanics, so I use a subjective central interval of about -1.0/+1.2 percentage points around the point: 11.6 to 13.8.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-16-46z-statjp-household-spending-real-yoy-two-or-more-person-households-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/parsed_cells.json:45:      \"One-off calendar and bonus-timing effects can move the monthly household survey materially\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-16-46z-statjp-household-spending-real-yoy-two-or-more-person-households-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/cells.with_activity.json:45:      \"One-off calendar and bonus-timing effects can move the monthly household survey materially\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/manifest.json:58:        \"summary\": \"The 80% interval is asserted as roughly -1.0/+1.2 points but is not tied to realized volatility, backtest error, survey error, or a stated subjective uncertainty calculation.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/parsed_cells.json:48:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/parsed_cells.json:94:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: if 2025 shelter and medical out-of-pocket adjustments run hotter than cash-income gains, or if survey income measurement weakens, the rate could stay near 13.5 or above despite continued employment growth.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/parsed_cells.json:98:        \"text\": \"Point estimate: start from the 2022-2024 average (12.4+12.9+12.9)/3=12.73, apply only a small -0.1 persistence-plus-mild-income adjustment, and round to 12.6. For the 80% interval, recent non-pandemic-transfer year-to-year moves include 2022 to 2023 of +0.5 and 2023 to 2024 of 0.0, but SPM is sensitive to thresholds, expenses, survey measurement, and policy mechanics, so I use a subjective central interval of about -1.0/+1.2 percentage points around the point: 11.6 to 13.8.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-16-46z-statjp-household-spending-real-yoy-two-or-more-person-households-may-2026-first-print-may-2026/normalized_cells.json:45:   \"One-off calendar and bonus-timing effects can move the monthly household survey materially\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:48:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:94:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: if 2025 shelter and medical out-of-pocket adjustments run hotter than cash-income gains, or if survey income measurement weakens, the rate could stay near 13.5 or above despite continued employment growth.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:98:        \"text\": \"Point estimate: start from the 2022-2024 average (12.4+12.9+12.9)/3=12.73, apply only a small -0.1 persistence-plus-mild-income adjustment, and round to 12.6. For the 80% interval, recent non-pandemic-transfer year-to-year moves include 2022 to 2023 of +0.5 and 2023 to 2024 of 0.0, but SPM is sensitive to thresholds, expenses, survey measurement, and policy mechanics, so I use a subjective central interval of about -1.0/+1.2 percentage points around the point: 11.6 to 13.8.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-47-15z-census-spm-all-people-poverty-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:367:          \"summary\": \"The 80% interval is asserted as roughly -1.0/+1.2 points but is not tied to realized volatility, backtest error, survey error, or a stated subjective uncertainty calculation.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/manifest.json:58:        \"summary\": \"The 80% interval is justified mainly from two recent annual changes plus qualitative survey/category noise, which is a weak realized-volatility basis.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/parsed_cells.json:15:    \"resolutionSourceUrl\": \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/health-insurance/time-series/hic/hhi01.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/parsed_cells.json:47:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/health-insurance/time-series/hic/hhi01.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/parsed_cells.json:108:        \"text\": \"Base 2024 rate 10.8 + expected 2025 net increase 0.5 = 11.3. The 80 percent interval is judgmental rather than a clean long-history volatility estimate: recent annual changes of +0.3 and +0.6, survey/category noise, and policy churn support a +/-0.6 point band, giving 10.7 to 11.9.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:15:    \"resolutionSourceUrl\": \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/health-insurance/time-series/hic/hhi01.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:47:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/health-insurance/time-series/hic/hhi01.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:108:        \"text\": \"Base 2024 rate 10.8 + expected 2025 net increase 0.5 = 11.3. The 80 percent interval is judgmental rather than a clean long-history volatility estimate: recent annual changes of +0.3 and +0.6, survey/category noise, and policy churn support a +/-0.6 point band, giving 10.7 to 11.9.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/cells.with_activity.json:377:          \"summary\": \"The 80% interval is justified mainly from two recent annual changes plus qualitative survey/category noise, which is a weak realized-volatility basis.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/normalized_cells.json:15:  \"resolutionSourceUrl\": \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/health-insurance/time-series/hic/hhi01.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/normalized_cells.json:47:   \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/health-insurance/time-series/hic/hhi01.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t13-42-20z-census-asec-direct-purchase-coverage-rate-2025-calendar-year-2025/normalized_cells.json:108:    \"text\": \"Base 2024 rate 10.8 + expected 2025 net increase 0.5 = 11.3. The 80 percent interval is judgmental rather than a clean long-history volatility estimate: recent annual changes of +0.3 and +0.6, survey/category noise, and policy churn support a +/-0.6 point band, giving 10.7 to 11.9.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
