ure near the July survey month.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-38-59z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-07/cells.with_activity.json:110:        \"text\": \"Prior/update/interval: I use a persistence plus short-moving-average model prior built from the February-June first-print sample, then adjust for latest revised momentum, low claims, the June one-off leisure weakness, and the softer 12-month BLS average; the 80% interval is sized from the BLS stated establishment-survey monthly-change sampling uncertainty of roughly +/-122 thousand at 90 percent, widened to +/-150 thousand for first-print and turning-point risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:82:    \"call\": \"ABS Labour Force survey-modernisation quality-assurance article\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:95:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: the June survey period, 31 May to 13 June, could still catch slower hiring or higher participation, and the ABS modernisation transition runs through April-August 2026. Those factors keep a 4.7 or 4.8 print in the 80 percent upper tail even though the modal rounded print remains 4.4.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-42-24z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:103:    \"text\": \"Upside scenario: a renewed participation rise or weaker June employment gain pushes the rounded rate to 4.6-4.8. Downside scenario: May's employment strength persists and unemployment falls back toward 4.2-4.3. Outside-the-interval scenarios are a survey-noise or labour-demand shock producing 4.0 or lower, or a sharp rise in unemployed people producing 4.9 or higher.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-03-35z-abs-labour-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:85:    \"call\": \"ABS trend and survey-timing context from April 2026 Labour Force 