mponents are -0.14 million for weak June payrolls/revisions, -0.01 million for lower May hires, and +0.01 million for still-low unemployment, giving 7.45 million after rounding. Interval method uses realized JOLTS first-print monthly dispersion around 0.35 to 0.40 million, widened to about +/-0.60 million for survey noise and the elevated May level, implying 6.85 to 8.05 million.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t21-18-26z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:104:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration and falsification: upside risk is that employers keep vacancies posted even while slowing fills, which could keep openings above 7.9 million. Downside risk is that the weak June payroll print reflects a broad pullback in postings, pulling openings toward 6.9 million. A shock or survey-noise print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million would land outside the interval.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-06-42z-statjp-cpi-tokyo-all-items-yoy-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:68:    \"result\": \"Schedule shows Ku-area of Tokyo preliminary: survey month May 2026 release May 29 and survey month June 2026 release June 26; same schedule line shows Japan May release June 19.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-07-04/2026-07-04t19-11-22z-bls-jolts-job-openings-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:114:    \"text\": \"Upside scenario: openings stay near or above 7.9 million if employers keep requisitions open despite slower hiring and labor-supply constraints bind in services. Downside scenario: openings fall toward 6.8 million if June's payroll slowdown reflects a rapid pullback in vacancy posting. Outside-the-interval scenario: a survey-noise or broad-demand shock print below 6.85 million or above 8.05 million is possible but less likely than normal JOLTS volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-06-42z-statjp-cpi-tokyo-all-items-yoy-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:68:        \"result\": \"Schedule shows Ku-area of Tokyo preliminary