on.json:2208757:            \"The June forecast stays near the May 2026 CPS table value because this monthly household-survey row is noisy and not seasonally adjusted. The point allows a small recovery from May but keeps the interval wide enough for sampling movement and month-specific occupation coding shifts.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2241085:            \"The advance indicator points to a 0.6% April increase after March's 0.9% current-dollar gain. The interval allows the early estimate to revise because the advance response rate was well below the final survey response rate and March volume sales were weaker than nominal sales.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2241103:            \"The advance indicator points to a 0.6% April increase after March's 0.9% current-dollar gain. The interval allows the early estimate to revise because the advance response rate was well below the final survey response rate and March volume sales were weaker than nominal sales.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2242336:            \"Direct-purchase coverage is the Census-side counterpart to marketplace enrollment. It tests whether agents can reconcile administrative sign-ups with survey-reported insurance categories.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2242431:            \"Base 2024 rate 10.8 + expected 2025 net increase 0.5 = 11.3. The 80 percent interval is judgmental rather than a clean long-history volatility estimate: recent annual changes of +0.3 and +0.6, survey/category noise, and policy churn support a +/-0.6 point band, giving 10.7 to 11.9.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2269227:            \"Tool result: The official schedule lists Japan survey month May with date of release June 19, 2026; the page last update is 23 January 2026.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2271765:            \"Tool call: Checked the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the survey month.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2271974:            \"The rate has clustered at 4.3 percent, so the control repeats the modal value but keeps a wider interval for household-survey noise.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2271991:            \"The rate has clustered at 4.3 percent, so the control repeats the modal value but keeps a wider interval for household-survey noise.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272000:            \"The rate has clustered at 4.3 percent, so the control repeats the modal value but keeps a wider interval for household-survey noise.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272128:            \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272129:            \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272145:            \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272155:            \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272175:            \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272204:            \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272214:            \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272223:            \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\",\nrecords/target-architecture/2026-06-29-projection.json:2272234:            \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-04-41z-abs-labour-employment-change-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:41:      \"ABS flagged two incoming rotation groups for May, increasing survey-noise risk\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-04-41z-abs-labour-employment-change-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:92:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: April's unemployment-rate rise to 4.5% and the ABS note that May has two incoming rotation groups raise downside and survey-volatility risk, so I keep a wide interval that includes another negative employment change.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-06-11z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:79:        \"call\": \"Checked the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the survey month.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:93:    \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:97:    \"text\": \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-06-11z-bls-ces-nonfarm-payrolls-change-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:79:        \"call\": \"Checked the latest official DOL unemployment insurance weekly claims release for layoff pressure near the survey month.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:45:   \"Household-survey sampling noise can move the rounded rate by a few tenths\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:78:    \"call\": \"Read BLS archived May 2026 Employment Situation table A-1 for recent household-survey values.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:93:    \"text\": \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/normalized_cells.json:101:    \"text\": \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:93:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:97:        \"text\": \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:93:        \"text\": \"Counter-consideration: household-survey noise can move the rounded unemployment rate by 0.1 or 0.2 points even when payroll growth is solid, and long-term unemployment near 2.0 million leaves some upside risk.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-19-11z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:97:        \"text\": \"Average of Jan-May rounded rates is (4.3 + 4.4 + 4.3 + 4.3 + 4.3) / 5 = 4.32, rounded to a 4.3 percent point forecast. I set the 80 percent interval at 4.1 to 4.6 to allow roughly minus 0.2 to plus 0.3 points around the recent 4.3 percent center, with slightly more upside due to household-survey volatility.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:16:    \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolve to the first Statistics Bureau of Japan Labour Force Survey Basic Tabulation May 2026 seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for total persons aged 15 years or over, as first released on the official monthly results/survey results page on June 30, 2026. Use the agency's published percent value rounded to one decimal place; ignore later revisions or historical-data back revisions for this forecast cell.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:67:        \"call\": \"Checked the Statistics Bureau Labour Force Survey landing and survey-results pages for the official series surface and latest monthly release status.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/cells.with_activity.json:68:        \"result\": \"The official page says fiscal 2026 release schedule was published on 2026-01-30; the survey-results page lists April 2026 monthly Basic Tabulation released on 2026-05-29; e-Stat table 1-a-1 covers major items including Unemployment rate, survey date 2026Apr, update date 2026-05-29, since Jan. 1953.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-33-47z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-july-to-september-2026-first-print-jul-sep-2026/normalized_cells.json:107:    \"text\": \"Policy and measurement mechanism: the target is a household-survey unemployment rate, not a claimant-count or payroll series. Soft PAYE and vacancies tend to lift unemployment with a lag, but seasonal-adjustment review and LFS sampling variability can move the first print by several tenths without a true labour-market break.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:45:      \"Household-survey sampling noise can move the rounded rate by a few tenths\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:78:        \"call\": \"Read BLS archived May 2026 Employment Situation table A-1 for recent household-survey values.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:93:        \"text\": \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/parsed_cells.json:101:        \"text\": \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:16:  \"resolutionRule\": \"Resolve to the first Statistics Bureau of Japan Labour Force Survey Basic Tabulation May 2026 seasonally adjusted unemployment rate for total persons aged 15 years or over, as first released on the official monthly results/survey results page on June 30, 2026. Use the agency's published percent value rounded to one decimal place; ignore later revisions or historical-data back revisions for this forecast cell.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:67:    \"call\": \"Checked the Statistics Bureau Labour Force Survey landing and survey-results pages for the official series surface and latest monthly release status.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-17/2026-06-17t02-09-00z-statjp-lfs-unemployment-rate-2026-05/normalized_cells.json:68:    \"result\": \"The official page says fiscal 2026 release schedule was published on 2026-01-30; the survey-results page lists April 2026 monthly Basic Tabulation released on 2026-05-29; e-Stat table 1-a-1 covers major items including Unemployment rate, survey date 2026Apr, update date 2026-05-29, since Jan. 1953.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:48:   \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/normalized_cells.json:50:   \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:45:      \"Household-survey sampling noise can move the rounded rate by a few tenths\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:78:        \"call\": \"Read BLS archived May 2026 Employment Situation table A-1 for recent household-survey values.\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:93:        \"text\": \"The main upside risk is that the household survey can be noisy and BLS says the unemployment-rate monthly-change confidence interval is about plus or minus 0.3 percentage point near a 6.0 percent rate; a softer June household survey could round to 4.4 or 4.5 even if payrolls remain positive.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-21/2026-06-21t15-07-16z-bls-cps-unemployment-rate-2026-06/cells.with_activity.json:101:        \"text\": \"Use persistence as the anchor: median of Jan-May rounded rates = 4.3. Apply no drift because May payrolls were strong and participation was flat, but widen the 80 percent interval to roughly one BLS household-survey rounded-rate move below and above: 4.1 to 4.6.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:48:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/parsed_cells.json:50:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:48:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/287/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t14-15-42z-census-spm-child-poverty-rate-2027-calendar-year-2027/cells.with_activity.json:50:      \"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/demo/tables/p60/283/tableB-2.xlsx\",\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-33-47z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-july-to-september-2026-first-print-jul-sep-2026/parsed_cells.json:107:        \"text\": \"Policy and measurement mechanism: the target is a household-survey unemployment rate, not a claimant-count or payroll series. Soft PAYE and vacancies tend to lift unemployment with a lag, but seasonal-adjustment review and LFS sampling variability can move the first print by several tenths without a true labour-market break.\"\nrecords/thesis-analyst/2026-06-27/2026-06-27t23-33-47z-ons-labour-unemployment-rate-gma = 1.28*0.149 = 0.191 million, implying bounds 7.559 +/- 0.191 = [7.368, 7.750].\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Upside risk is an abrupt rebound like April 2026, which could put the print above 7.750 million. Downside risk is a July seasonal drop materially larger than 2025's 0.276 million or renewed manufacturing weakness; a decline exceeding 0.391 million from June would land below the interval. Either outcome would be outside the interval.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Review disposition: Accepted canonical Table A-19 resolver wording, explicit treatment of the August 7 official-calendar discrepancy, inclusion of June 2025 context, and characterization of the -0.200 million update as judgmental shrinkage from a limited seasonal sample.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":7.559,\"ciLow\":7.368,\"ciHigh\":7.75}]}"}}
