es/empsit_06052026.htm","https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_05082026.htm","https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_08012025.pdf","https://www.bls.gov/schedule/2026/home.htm"],"runAt":"2026-07-21T01:12:14Z","reasoning":[{"kind":"heading","text":"July 2026 CPS production-occupation employment forecast"},{"kind":"text","text":"The target is the first BLS print for total people employed in Production occupations, July 2026, not seasonally adjusted, from CPS Employment Situation Table A-13/A-19. BLS reports thousands; the resolver multiplies by 0.001. Later revisions do not replace the first print."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Read BLS archived Employment Situation releases for January through March 2026, Table A-13.","result":"Fetched first-print production-occupation employment of 7,905 thousand in January 2026, 7,742 thousand in February, and 7,685 thousand in March."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Read BLS archived Employment Situation releases for April through June 2026, Table A-13.","result":"Fetched first-print production-occupation employment of 7,883 thousand in April 2026, 7,912 thousand in May, and 7,759 thousand in June."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Read the archived July 2025 Employment Situation, Table A-13, for a same-month seasonal comparison.","result":"Fetched July production-occupation employment of 7,889 thousand in 2024 and 7,662 thousand in 2025; the June 2025 official table value was 7,938 thousand, implying a 276-thousand June-to-July decline in 2025."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Verify the July 2026 Employment Situation publication date using the official BLS release calendar and June 2026 release.","result":"The official calendar lists Friday, August 7, 2026 at 8:30 AM ET, and the June release repeats August 7, 2026; this is 1 day after the ledger resolutionDate of August 6."},{"kind":"text","text":"The reference class/base rate is persistence around the latest NSA CPS occupation level with a negative July seasonal update. The January-June sequence is choppy but centered near 7.8 million, while July 2025 fell 0.276 million from June and remained 0.227 million below July 2024."},{"kind":"math","text":"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = June 2026 first print of 7.759 million; historical sample = January-June 2026 first prints [7.905, 7.742, 7.685, 7.883, 7.912, 7.759]. Successive changes are [-0.163, -0.057, +0.198, +0.029, -0.153] million, whose sample standard deviation is sigma = 0.149 million. Apply a -0.200 million update for July seasonality and weak year-over-year level momentum, with no separate policy adjustment: 7.759 - 0.200 = 7.559. The normal 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.149 = 0.191 million, implying bounds 7.559 +/- 0.191 = [7.368, 7.750]."},{"kind":"text","text":"Upside risk is an abrupt rebound like April 2026, which could put the print above 7.750 million. Downside risk is a July seasonal drop materially larger than 2025's 0.276 million or renewed manufacturing weakness; a decline exceeding 0.391 million from June would land below the interval. Either outcome would be outside the interval."},{"kind":"forecast","point":7.559,"ciLow":7.368,"ciHigh":7.75}]}
./records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-21/2026-07-21t01-04-32z-bls-cps-employed-people-by-occupation-business-financial-operations-2026-07/raw_response.txt:1:{"slug":"cps-business-financial-employment-july-2026","country":"US","type":"data","title":"US business and financial operations employment, July 2026","question":"What will BLS first report for employed people in business and financial operations occupations in July 2026, not seasonally adjusted, in millions?","unit":"millions","pointEstimate":9.69,"ciLow":9.35,"ciHigh":10.03,"confidence":0.8,"resolutionDate":"2026-08-07","resolutionSource":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics Employment Situation, Table A-19","resolutionSourceUrl":"https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cpseea19.htm","resolutionRule":"Resolve to the first value published by BLS for July 2026 in CPS Employment Situation Table A-19, row 'Business and financial operations occupations,' Total age 16 years and over, not seasonally adjusted, reported in thousands and multiplied by 0.001 to millions. Use the first official print without later revisions. The official BLS calendar schedules the July release for August 7, 2026; this conflicts with the registered sourceBinding window ending August 6 but does not change the target.","dataPointId":"bls.cps.employed_people_by_occupation.business_financial_operations.july_2026.first_print","historicalContext":[{"label":"June 2025 NSA","value":10.205},{"label":"April 2026 NSA","value":9.857},{"label":"June 2026 NSA","value":9.72},{"label":"2024 annual average","value":9.945}],"drivers":["June 2026 level persistence","recent downward occupational-employment observations","CPS occupational sampling volatility","July seasonal composition"],"sourceContext":["https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cpseea19.htm","https://www.bls.gov/schedule/2026/home.htm","https://www.bls.gov/cps/publications/release-calendar.htm","https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cpseea44.pdf","https://www.bls.gov/opub/reports/womens-databook/"],"runAt":"2026-07-21T01:05:05Z","reasoning":[{"kind":"heading","text":"July 2026 business and financial operations employment forecast"},{"kind":"text","text":"The resolver is the first July 2026 print in BLS CPS Table A-19 for Business and financial operations occupations, total age 16 years and over, not seasonally adjusted. Table values are thousands and are converted to millions by multiplying by 0.001."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Fetch the latest BLS CPS Employment Situation Table A-19 values for the exact NSA occupational row.","result":"BLS Table A-19 reports 9,720 thousand in June 2026 and 10,205 thousand in June 2025, equal to 9.720 million and 10.205 million."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Fetch an earlier exact-row observation from BLS CPS occupational tables.","result":"BLS Table A-44 reports 9,857 thousand people in Business and financial operations occupations in April 2026, equal to 9.857 million."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Fetch the BLS annual occupation reference value.","result":"The BLS Women in the Labor Force occupation table reports 9,945 thousand employed in Business and financial operations occupations for the 2024 annual average, equal to 9.945 million."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Verify the July 2026 Employment Situation publication date on official BLS calendars.","result":"The BLS 2026 release schedule and CPS calendar both list the July 2026 Employment Situation for August 7, 2026 at 8:30 a.m. ET; the registered expected window ends August 6, one day too early."},{"kind":"text","text":"The reference class and base rate are persistence around the latest exact-series level, with substantial CPS sampling noise. The official observations show a lower June 2026 level than June 2025, while the 2024 annual average was 9.945 million."},{"kind":"text","text":"Level effect: anchor at June's 9.720 million. Momentum effect: the observed sequence from 10.205 to 9.857 to 9.720 is negative. One-off effect: noisy CPS occupational allocation argues against extrapolating the full decline. Policy mechanism: no discrete policy change was identified, so only a small judgmental negative adjustment is applied."},{"kind":"math","text":"Prior/update/interval: persistence model prior = 9.720 million; historical sample = 10.205, 9.857, and 9.720 million. Successive observed changes are -0.348 and -0.137 million, giving RMS sigma = sqrt((0.348^2 + 0.137^2)/2) = 0.264 million. Apply a -0.030 million momentum adjustment and no separate one-off or policy adjustment, yielding 9.690 million. The 80% half-width is approximately 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.264 = 0.338 million, rounded to 0.34, implying 9.35 to 10.03 million. A longer consistent monthly or June-to-July exact-row sample was unavailable in the fetched pre-resolution evidence, so this thin, irregular two-change proxy is retained as a judgmental uncertainty estimate rather than presented as a robust seasonal-volatility estimate."},{"kind":"text","text":"Upside risk is a rebound in sampled professional hiring or July occupational reclassification, which would land above 10.03 million. Downside risk is continued labor-market weakness combined with adverse CPS sampling, which would land below 9.35 million and outside the interval."},{"kind":"text","text":"Review disposition: Accepted the interval critique by explicitly identifying the sample's irregularity and defending it as a judgmental proxy because a longer consistent sample was unavailable. Accepted the suggestion to soften the unsupported financial-activities claim and retained the official-calendar discrepancy."},{"kind":"forecast","point":9.69,"ciLow":9.35,"ciHigh":10.03}]}
