./records/thesis-analyst/2026-07-21/2026-07-21t01-08-26z-bls-cps-employed-people-by-occupation-healthcare-support-2026-07/revision_prompt.md:132:{"slug":"cps-healthcare-support-employment-july-2026","country":"US","type":"data","title":"US healthcare support employment, July 2026","question":"What will BLS first report for July 2026 employed people age 16 and over in healthcare support occupations, not seasonally adjusted, in millions?","unit":"millions","pointEstimate":5.801,"ciLow":4.626,"ciHigh":6.976,"confidence":0.8,"resolutionDate":"2026-08-07","resolutionSource":"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics Employment Situation, CPS Table A-19","resolutionSourceUrl":"https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cpseea19.htm","resolutionRule":"Resolve to the first value published for July 2026 in BLS CPS Employment Situation Table A-19, row 'Healthcare support occupations,' Total, 16 years and over, not seasonally adjusted. Multiply the published thousands value by 0.001 and retain three decimal places in millions. Ignore all later revisions; no correction or release-day grace exception applies.","dataPointId":"bls.cps.employed_people_by_occupation.healthcare_support.july_2026.first_print","historicalContext":[{"label":"July 2023 NSA CPS healthcare support employment","value":4.911},{"label":"June 2025 NSA CPS healthcare support employment","value":5.95},{"label":"June 2026 NSA CPS healthcare support employment","value":5.691}],"drivers":["June 2026 level persistence","modest July seasonal uplift","long-run healthcare labor demand","high CPS subgroup sampling volatility"],"sourceContext":["https://www.bls.gov/web/empsit/cpseea19.htm","https://www.bls.gov/schedule/2026/home.htm","https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_07022026.pdf","https://www.bls.gov/news.release/conemp.t02.htm"],"runAt":"2026-07-21T01:09:11Z","reasoning":[{"kind":"heading","text":"July 2026 healthcare support employment forecast"},{"kind":"text","text":"The resolver is the first July 2026 print for Total employed people age 16 and over in 'Healthcare support occupations' in CPS Table A-19, measured in thousands and not seasonally adjusted, then multiplied by 0.001. All anchors below use that same CPS occupation/NSA variant. The ledger expectedReleaseWindow ending 2026-08-06 conflicts with the official August 7 release date; the forecast remains tied to the registered target and uses the verified official date."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Read current BLS CPS Employment Situation Table A-19 for the healthcare support occupations row.","result":"BLS Table A-19 reports 5,950 thousand for June 2025 and 5,691 thousand for June 2026, equivalent to 5.950 million and 5.691 million."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Read the BLS July 2023 contingent-work table as an additional public CPS occupation-level reference observation.","result":"The BLS CPS table reports 4,911 thousand total employed people in healthcare support occupations in July 2023, equivalent to 4.911 million."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.calendar","call":"Verify the July 2026 Employment Situation publication date using the BLS 2026 release calendar and latest release announcement.","result":"The official calendar lists Employment Situation for July 2026 on Friday, August 7, 2026 at 8:30 AM ET; the June 2026 release repeats August 7, 2026."},{"kind":"text","text":"The outside-view base rate/reference class is persistence around the latest 5.691 million level, with substantial CPS subgroup noise. The sparse same-variant official history spans 4.911 million in July 2023, 5.950 million in June 2025, and 5.691 million in June 2026."},{"kind":"text","text":"Level contributes 5.691 million. Momentum is mixed: the latest year-over-year change is -0.259 million. A July calendar/seasonal adjustment contributes +0.080 million, while continuing structural demand for healthcare support labor contributes +0.030 million; no discrete policy or one-off shock is added."},{"kind":"math","text":"Prior/update/interval: persistence model prior = 5.691 million; historical sample = 4.911, 5.950, and 5.691 million. Successive changes are +1.039 and -0.259 million; their sample standard deviation gives sigma = sqrt(((1.039-0.390)^2+(-0.259-0.390)^2)/(2-1)) = 0.918 million. Updates are +0.080 July seasonality and +0.030 structural healthcare demand, so point = 5.691+0.080+0.030 = 5.801 million. The 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.918 = 1.175 million, implying 5.801-1.175 = 4.626 and 5.801+1.175 = 6.976 million."},{"kind":"text","text":"Upside risk comes from unusually strong household-survey sampling, labor-force entry, or faster caregiving hiring and would land above the interval at more than 6.976 million. Downside risk comes from a sharp participation decline, healthcare funding disruption, or an adverse CPS sampling swing and would land below the interval at less than 4.626 million. The wide interval reflects realized occupation-level dispersion rather than a rounded hedge."},{"kind":"forecast","point":5.801,"ciLow":4.626,"ciHigh":6.976}]}
